  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "RFR8vyqIkcPHzGRL49VYEjPiqLo=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:28:38.465Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "Devapo is the lead that tests the other hypothesis. It is Camunda-certified with 7+ years in banking, finance and insurance, and small enough to move quickly, but it is UK finance rather than sovereign public sector. An engagement here exercises the claims-processing argument rather than the digital-sovereignty argument.\n\nThat is a feature of the lead set rather than a defect in the lead. Most of the Camunda and Common Ground names support the same story: European public bodies needing verifiable records under their own control. Devapo has the same orchestration gap without the sovereignty motive, so it isolates whether the append-only-record pitch stands on operational grounds alone \u2014 audit, dispute and replay inside claims handling \u2014 with the sovereignty framing removed.\n\nIts size helps that test. A small firm can run a pilot on a decision rather than a procurement, so the hypothesis gets exercised cheaply. It sits at priority 3 because it is a test of the argument rather than a route into the primary market.\n\n---\n\n## Leads sharing this finding (1)\n\n**3 \u00b7 later**\n- Devapo (UK \u00b7 small)\n\nVerbatim research text this claim was extracted from:\n\n> Camunda certified 7+ yrs in banking, finance and INSURANCE. Small enough to move fast. But UK finance, not sovereign public sector - so it tests the claims-processing route rather than the sovereignty route. Useful precisely because it tests the other hypothesis.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-19T11:28:38.465333Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:28:38.465338Z"
    }
  },
